{number: 33}
{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{key: C}

{start_of_verse: 1}
When [C] I survey the [F] wondrous [C] cross
On which the [Am] Prince of [G] glory [C] died,
My richest gain I [F] count but [C] loss,
And pour con-[Am] tempt on [G7] all my [C] pride.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 2}
For-[C] bid it, Lord, that [F] I should [C] boast,
Save in the [Am] cross of [G] Christ, my [C] God:
All the vain things that [F] charm me [C] most,
I sacrifice [Am] them to [G7] His [C] blood.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
[C] See, from His head, His [F] hands, His [C] feet,
Sorrow and [Am] love flow [G] mingled [C] down!
Did e'er such love and [F] sorrow [C] meet?
Or thorns com-[Am] pose so [G7] rich a [C] crown?
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
Were [C] the whole realm of [F] nature [C] mine,
That were a [Am] tribute [G] far too [C] small;
Love so amazing, [F] so di-[C] vine,
Demands my [Am] soul, my [G7] life, my [C] all.
{end_of_verse}
